-          "excerpt": "Input $N\\ge1$, $x\\in\\mathbb C^N$, and finite phase/arithmetic budgets. The output convention is the **unnormalised** DFT $X_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}x_je^{-2\\pi ijk/N}$ ([[def-unnormalised-engineering-dft-and-conversion]]); the procedure returns an approximate vector or the first `inconclusive`/`range-failure` status.\n\n1. For $N=1$, return $x_0$.\n2. If $N=2^m$ with $m\\ge1$, call [[alg-na-computed-unitary-radix-two-fft]] in `forward` mode and multiply each output by the computed scalar $\\operatorname{fl}(\\sqrt N)$. A failed subcall or scale operation returns its status.\n3. If $N$ is composite and not a power of two, find its least divisor $r$ with $1<r<N$ by trial division and put $s=N/r$. For $u=0,\\ldots,r-1$, recursively transform the strided vector $(x_{u+rv})_{v=0}^{s-1}$ to obtain an approximate unnormalised length-$s$ DFT $U_u$. For each $0\\le k_2<s$, approximate every twiddle $e^{-2\\pi iuk_2/N}$ by [[alg-na-certified-unit-circle-phase]], multiply it by $U_u(k_2)$ with the operationwise complex product, and apply the recursive length-$r$ transform to the resulting vector indexed by $u$. Store its output $k_1$ at index $k_2+sk_1$. Propagate any failed phase or arithmetic status.\n4. If $N$ is prime, set $M$ to the least power of two with $M\\ge2N-1$. Form the chirp-modulated input $a_j=\\alpha_jx_j$ with $\\alpha_j=e^{-\\pi i j^2/N}$ and the length-$M$ chirp vector $b$ supported on residues of $t=-(N-1),\\ldots,N-1$, where $b_{[t]_M}=e^{\\pi i t^2/N}$. Obtain each phase with [[alg-na-certified-unit-circle-phase]], compute the cyclic convolution with [[alg-na-computed-fft-cyclic-and-linear-convolution]] in `cyclic` mode, and multiply output $k<N$ by $\\gamma_k=e^{-\\pi i k^2/N}$, again using a certified phase and the rounded complex product. Return the first $N$ entries or the first failure status.\n\nThe divisor search and phase-enclosure work have separate integer/interval budgets and are not counted as complex arithmetic operations in the operation-count theorem. The exact factorization lemmas and the chirp-phase error theorem state what is guaranteed; this procedure by itself makes no general mixed-radix stability claim.",
+          "excerpt": "Input $N\\ge1$, $x\\in\\mathbb C^N$, a phase tolerance $0<\\tau\\le1/4$, integer dyadic exponent limits $e_{\\min}\\le e_{\\max}$, an initial phase precision $p_0\\ge2$, a finite precision cap $p_{\\max}\\ge p_0$, a finite elementary-series cap $J\\ge1$, and a maximum radix-two recursion depth $d_{\\max}\\ge0$. At precision $p$, phase endpoints use the dyadic format with precision $p$ and exponent limits $e_{\\min},e_{\\max}$. The output convention is the **unnormalised** DFT $X_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}x_je^{-2\\pi ijk/N}$ ([[def-unnormalised-engineering-dft-and-conversion]]); the procedure returns an approximate vector or the first `inconclusive`/`range-failure` status.\n\n1. For $N=1$, return $x_0$.\n2. If $N=2^m$ with $m\\ge1$, call [[alg-na-computed-unitary-radix-two-fft]] in `forward` mode with controls $(\\tau,e_{\\min},e_{\\max},p_0,p_{\\max},J,d_{\\max})$ and multiply each output by the computed scalar $\\operatorname{fl}(\\sqrt N)$. A failed subcall or scale operation returns its status.\n3. If $N$ is composite and not a power of two, find its least divisor $r$ with $1<r<N$ by trial division and put $s=N/r$. For $u=0,\\ldots,r-1$, recursively call this procedure with the same controls on the strided vector $(x_{u+rv})_{v=0}^{s-1}$ to obtain an approximate unnormalised length-$s$ DFT $U_u$. For each $0\\le k_2<s$ and $0\\le u<r$, call [[alg-na-certified-unit-circle-phase]] on $(q,\\tau,e_{\\min},e_{\\max},p_0,p_{\\max},J)$ with $q=-2uk_2/N$ to obtain the twiddle enclosure, then multiply its returned phase by $U_u(k_2)$ using $\\operatorname{fl}_{\\mathbb C}$. Apply this procedure recursively, with the same controls, to the resulting vector indexed by $u$ at length $r$. Store its output $k_1$ at index $k_2+sk_1$. Propagate any failed phase or arithmetic status.\n4. If $N$ is prime, set $M$ to the least power of two with $M\\ge2N-1$. For each $0\\le j<N$, call [[alg-na-certified-unit-circle-phase]] on $(q,\\tau,e_{\\min},e_{\\max},p_0,p_{\\max},J)$ with $q=-j^2/N$ to obtain $\\widehat\\alpha_j$, and set $a_j=\\operatorname{fl}_{\\mathbb C}(\\widehat\\alpha_jx_j)$; set the remaining entries of $a\\in\\mathbb C^M$ to zero. For each $-(N-1)\\le t\\le N-1$, obtain $\\widehat\\beta_t$ by the same call with $q=t^2/N$ and set $b_{[t]_M}=\\widehat\\beta_t$, setting other entries of $b$ to zero. Compute the cyclic convolution with [[alg-na-computed-fft-cyclic-and-linear-convolution]] in `cyclic` mode using controls $(\\tau,e_{\\min},e_{\\max},p_0,p_{\\max},J,d_{\\max})$. For each $0\\le k<N$, obtain $\\widehat\\gamma_k$ by the same phase call with $q=-k^2/N$ and set $\\widehat X_k=\\operatorname{fl}_{\\mathbb C}(\\widehat\\gamma_k\\widehat c_k)$. Return the first $N$ entries or the first failure status.\n\nThe divisor search and phase-enclosure work have separate integer/interval budgets and are not counted as complex arithmetic operations in the operation-count theorem. The exact factorization lemmas and the chirp-phase error theorem state what is guaranteed; this procedure by itself makes no general mixed-radix stability claim.",
